\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced G-MFRM, a Bayesian measurement model that jointly estimates model ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility within a single inferential framework. The key theoretical contribution is formalizing the confounding between judge bias and benchmark contamination as omitted variable bias: omitting either facet produces a closed-form bias in the other, with direction and magnitude determined by the exposure--severity covariance (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

On Arena-Hard, G-MFRM achieves nominally higher rank agreement with human preferences ($\tau = 0.857$ vs.\ $0.786$; $P(\Delta\tau \geq 0) = 0.927$, not significant at $N = 8$), while LOO cross-validation strongly favors the joint model ($|\Delta\mathrm{ELPD}/\mathrm{SE}| = 5.9$, stacking weight 1.0). The contamination susceptibility profile reveals a clear gradient by model age, with older models showing 4--6$\times$ higher susceptibility, and the bias absorption analysis confirms that MFRM-only inflates ability estimates for contaminated models by up to 0.79 logits. Ablation identifies joint estimation as the dominant contributor: staged estimation doubles ability RMSE, while continuous exposure gating improves contamination recovery by 40\% over binary.

Two practical implications emerge. First, evaluation frameworks that treat judge bias and contamination as independent---the current default---incur systematic errors that grow with the heterogeneity of model contamination levels; future LLM evaluation should model these facets jointly. Second, judge panels benefit from severity diversity: leave-one-judge-out analysis shows that judges at the severity extremes are individually necessary for the ranking improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:judge_count}), suggesting that panel composition matters as much as panel size.

Several directions remain open. The rank-1 contamination structure assumes a single $c_n$ per model; domain-specific contamination (e.g., coding vs.\ reasoning) motivates higher-rank decompositions $\sum_r c_{nr} \cdot e_{ir}$. Posterior credible intervals for $c_n$ exhibit systematic overconfidence (71\% coverage at nominal 95\%); simulation-based calibration \citep{talts2018sbc} offers a path to corrected uncertainty quantification. Scaling G-MFRM to larger model populations and diverse benchmarks would strengthen the generalizability of the joint estimation advantage confirmed in synthetic experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:synthetic_calibration}).
